WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.821
Both tests follow the same three-step recipe:
choose a maximum subgraph order,

00:00:04.901 --> 00:00:08.905
estimate each sample's distribution from its
subgraph statistics,

00:00:08.985 --> 00:00:12.110
and measure the distance between the two estimates.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:15.251
That distance is the squared Mahalanobis distance,

00:00:15.331 --> 00:00:19.775
which weights differences by an analytically
computed covariance matrix.

00:00:19.855 --> 00:00:24.551
The only difference between the tests is the
coordinates: raw graph moments,

00:00:24.631 --> 00:00:29.326
which are injective-homomorphism subgraph
densities, versus graph cumulants.

00:00:29.406 --> 00:00:35.861
Cumulants are obtained from moments by a
combinatorial Möbius transform over all connectivity-respecting

00:00:35.941 --> 00:00:37.934
partitions of a subgraph's edges,

00:00:38.014 --> 00:00:43.840
capturing the excess propensity of a structure
beyond what its smaller pieces already predict.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:49.495
Because computing the covariances only needs
subgraph counts up to twice the chosen order,

00:00:49.575 --> 00:00:52.700
both tests share the same computational complexity.
